WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:45.000
[Music]

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:06.400
[Narrator:] The 18th century, the Age of Reason, was a time of hospital-building.

00:01:06.414 --> 00:01:18.147
[Music]

00:01:18.147 --> 00:01:24.740
[Narrator:] Trying to heal the sick, man in his ignorance had devised a reservoir of sepsis.

00:01:30.847 --> 00:01:36.540
A few perceptive physicians observed that uncleanliness, overcrowding,

00:01:36.547 --> 00:01:47.213
and failure to isolate infectious patients encouraged the spread of disease.

00:01:47.214 --> 00:01:52.280
Following Pasteur's discoveries in the mid-19th century, the realization came that

00:01:52.281 --> 00:02:01.781
such hospital conditions spread disease because they spread organisms.

00:02:01.781 --> 00:02:14.547
We began to grope for new techniques to control disease. The age of antisepsis had begun.

00:02:14.547 --> 00:02:22.547
Joseph Lister stands out among the pioneers of that century. It was he who said, "You must be able to see

00:02:22.547 --> 00:02:31.880
with your mental eye the septic ferments as distinctly as we see flies or other insects with a corporeal eye.

00:02:31.881 --> 00:02:36.347
If you can really see them in this distinct way with your intellectual eye,

00:02:36.347 --> 00:02:41.747
you can be properly on your guard against them."

00:02:41.747 --> 00:02:47.113
This was our temper when the 20th century came in.

00:02:47.114 --> 00:02:53.780
We had learned to see Lister's septic ferments with our mental eye.

00:02:53.781 --> 00:03:01.014
Our understanding had minimized cross- infection. We were on our guard.

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:11.580
And today? Since the therapeutic revolution of antibiotics, we rely too much on antibiotics and not enough on asepsis.

00:03:11.581 --> 00:03:13.914
Our guard is down.

00:03:13.914 --> 00:03:18.014
Liberal visiting privileges and division of labor have pyramided the number of people

00:03:18.014 --> 00:03:23.147
who contact the patient and crowd the corridors.

00:03:23.147 --> 00:03:34.080
Each exchanges bacteria with the patients and with each other.

00:03:34.081 --> 00:03:40.214
Once again, many of our hospitals are reservoirs of uncontrolled infection

00:03:40.214 --> 00:03:45.380
because of the complication of antibiotic resistance.

00:03:45.381 --> 00:03:48.981
And the source of hospital sepsis?

00:03:48.981 --> 00:04:02.747
Originally it is this, the infected patient.

00:04:02.747 --> 00:04:11.413
Mrs. Sarah Allen had had a ten year history of boils and pimples.

00:04:11.414 --> 00:04:18.080
She was hospitalized eventually to discover the cause of the continued skin sepsis.

00:04:18.081 --> 00:04:23.014
The staphylococci which caused her difficulty were alien to the hospital and it was this fact

00:04:23.014 --> 00:04:30.514
that made her useful in tracing the spread of infection.

00:04:30.514 --> 00:04:34.680
A single room was disinfected for this particular investigation.

00:04:34.681 --> 00:04:43.214
The walls were sprayed with germicidal detergent and squeegeed dry.

00:04:43.214 --> 00:04:49.714
The furniture was washed with germicide and the bedding sterilized.

00:04:49.714 --> 00:04:53.880
The floor was flooded with detergent germicide and the film of water

00:04:53.881 --> 00:04:56.547
picked up with a vacuum cleaner.

00:04:56.547 --> 00:05:02.713
After disinfection, cultures were taken of the bedding, floor and the air.

00:05:02.714 --> 00:05:12.214
The environment prepared to house Mrs. Allen's organisms was relatively aseptic.

00:05:12.214 --> 00:05:20.580
Cultures were taken again after 24 hours of occupancy.

00:05:20.581 --> 00:05:27.681
The technique of culturing the bedding was simply to press it against the medium.

00:05:27.681 --> 00:05:34.181
The floor was cultured by swabbing a given area.

00:05:34.181 --> 00:05:42.114
The septic solution was then mixed with a culture medium.

00:05:42.114 --> 00:05:49.547
The air was cultured by using a welled centrifuge which passes a major quantity of air into a tube.

00:05:49.547 --> 00:06:02.813
Centrifugal action throws dust and bacteria against the medium on the wall of the tube.

00:06:02.814 --> 00:06:17.647
These bacteria accumulated during the first 24 hours of occupancy.

00:06:17.647 --> 00:06:24.347
Phage typing identified the bacteria isolated in the experiment. The translucent spots in the fourth square

00:06:24.347 --> 00:06:31.980
of each upper row indicate that bacteria from all cultures were destroyed by phage 79.

00:06:31.981 --> 00:06:37.581
This confirmed the source of organisms as being Mrs. Allen.

00:06:37.581 --> 00:06:47.547
Cultures were taken on subsequent days.

00:06:47.547 --> 00:07:06.280
This was the appearance of the cultures taken on day 7.

00:07:06.281 --> 00:07:09.481
The rise in the count of bacteria per square inch on bedding from admission

00:07:09.481 --> 00:07:15.547
through the seventh day was this.

00:07:15.547 --> 00:07:22.780
The floor count per square centimeter.

00:07:22.781 --> 00:07:30.514
The air count per five cubic feet.

00:07:30.514 --> 00:07:37.714
There was no moisture in Mrs. Allen's surroundings and hence no place for organisms to multiply.

00:07:37.714 --> 00:07:44.680
The rise in bacteria during the seven days, therefore, represents an accumulation only.

00:07:44.681 --> 00:07:49.881
The air counts charted here are averages.

00:07:49.881 --> 00:07:56.147
However, throughout a given day, the variation during different activities is significant.

00:07:56.147 --> 00:08:00.213
An air sample taken while the bed is being made gives a better idea of the increase

00:08:00.214 --> 00:08:11.447
in the room's bacterial population.

00:08:11.447 --> 00:08:18.880
Bed-making contributes a remarkable number of organisms to the surrounding air.

00:08:18.881 --> 00:08:24.814
During the period of no activity the count was 11 bacteria per five cubic feet of air.

00:08:24.814 --> 00:08:29.380
During bed-making, 160 or more.

00:08:29.381 --> 00:08:42.447
Other cultures showed this rise and fall.

00:08:42.447 --> 00:08:52.947
Here the patient straightens her bedcovers. And the period of no activity in the evening.

00:08:52.947 --> 00:09:04.947
What part of the patient precisely is the source of the accumulating organisms?

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:31.214
Evidently Mrs. Allen's carbuncles are only local manifestations of systemic bacterial disease.

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:36.980
She is shedding bacteria from her whole person.

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:43.380
This becomes obvious when we culture the bathroom fixtures that she contacts.

00:09:49.847 --> 00:09:56.113
It becomes obvious when an infected patient is allowed to leave an isolation room.

00:10:10.414 --> 00:10:17.447
These are colonies of Mrs. Allen's organisms shed during the simple act of walking by.

00:10:17.447 --> 00:10:22.480
Ordinary dressings do not confine bacteria to the lesion.

00:10:22.481 --> 00:10:28.147
Moisture seeps through the gauze, evaporates, and leaves bacteria to be scattered,

00:10:28.147 --> 00:10:38.680
as shown by this culture of the outer surface of Mrs. Allen's dressing.

00:10:38.681 --> 00:10:45.014
When a dressing is handled carelessly, it isn't hard to visualize the spread of organisms

00:10:45.014 --> 00:10:51.947
to the hands, clothes, and nose of the examiner.

00:10:51.947 --> 00:10:56.947
The physician's actions should set the pattern for safe hospital practices

00:10:56.947 --> 00:11:07.747
each time he cares for his patient.

00:11:07.747 --> 00:11:14.813
Another major source of organisms is the spray from conversation and breathing.

00:11:14.814 --> 00:11:28.447
In slow motion, the word "pipette" looks like this.

00:11:28.447 --> 00:11:35.713
A conclusion of the investigation is this: the infected patient is the source of hospital sepsis,

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:39.860
a readily communicable disease.

00:11:42.447 --> 00:11:52.380
Isolation techniques, such as the use of prepackaged sterile dressings, masks, gowns,

00:11:52.381 --> 00:11:59.681
an ultraviolet curtain, disinfection of shoes and hands, are essential to keep the bacteria

00:11:59.681 --> 00:12:02.981
within the patient's room.

00:12:02.981 --> 00:12:13.581
If not properly confined, bacteria can travel from the source of infection by dozens of routes.

00:12:13.581 --> 00:12:18.714
They are carried by feet, hands, clothing, and noses.

00:12:18.714 --> 00:12:25.047
They circulate by soiled linen and improper ventilating systems. They are spread by blankets,

00:12:25.047 --> 00:12:31.847
dressings, pails and mops...by any article that enters and leaves the room.

00:12:31.847 --> 00:12:37.200
But the action of each of these spreaders is not simply that of carrying a few bacteria

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:41.420
from an infected patient to some other patient.

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:48.880
Each also becomes a reservoir and some of these reservoirs may become serious multipliers.

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:52.940
For instance, a culture of mop-water taken at the end of the day

00:12:53.240 --> 00:12:56.380
may not yield an overwhelming picture of sepsis,

00:12:58.300 --> 00:13:01.760
but the same water presents a different picture the next morning.

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:17.300
Other common multipliers are damp soiled linen, dressings, moist soap, bedside water,

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:22.460
the air-conditioning system, and the nasopharynx.

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:31.540
Every hospital is a complex of possible multipliers, reservoirs and spreaders of bacteria.

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:39.480
Consider one of the many possible trails, bacteria spread from nose to skin to clothing to floor,

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:43.600
where they can live for long periods in the dust.

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:58.840
The bacteria are picked up on a mop, where overnight they multiply.

00:13:58.847 --> 00:14:06.980
Bacteria from this rich new source are painted generously throughout the hospital.

00:14:06.981 --> 00:14:12.347
The film of water dries, the bacteria are scuffed into the air.

00:14:12.347 --> 00:14:16.947
They may be carried into the air- conditioning system.

00:14:16.947 --> 00:14:24.047
From here they are fed back into the entire ventilation complex.

00:14:24.047 --> 00:14:30.000
As these various processes go on with dozens of interwoven multipliers and spreaders,

00:14:30.260 --> 00:14:33.740
gradually there is created a septic environment.

00:14:33.747 --> 00:14:39.513
The problem of each hospital is to discover which multipliers and spreaders are responsible

00:14:39.514 --> 00:14:42.447
for sepsis in that particular hospital.

00:14:42.447 --> 00:14:47.040
Steps can then be taken to block the feedbacks at various points.

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:55.814
There are practices common to many hospitals that may be a source of trouble.

00:14:55.820 --> 00:15:02.060
Mopping is one. We will mop Mrs. Allen's floor from a pail actually being used

00:15:02.060 --> 00:15:05.340
in another part of the hospital on this day.

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:10.060
The water contains only the usual detergent used in this hospital.

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:23.380
A culture was taken from the floor when it was again dry.

00:15:23.381 --> 00:15:28.247
Here is a comparison with the earlier culture taken before mopping.

00:15:28.247 --> 00:15:32.680
Mops may leave more bacteria on the floor than they remove,

00:15:32.681 --> 00:15:38.947
because the usual germicide is not effective in large wads of textile.

00:15:38.947 --> 00:15:43.813
It is better to use a vacuum cleaner or treated cloth.

00:15:43.814 --> 00:15:49.347
Still better results are obtained if the floor can be flooded periodically with a detergent germicide

00:15:49.347 --> 00:15:54.080
which is picked up with a wet vacuum.

00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:59.380
After this treatment, a culture demonstrates that for practical purposes the floor is eliminated

00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:01.540
as a source of bacteria.

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:19.980
Once the floor is properly treated, the bed remains as the principal reservoir.

00:16:19.981 --> 00:16:31.914
A typical bacterial count showed top sheet 160, pillow 176, and bottom sheet 240.

00:16:31.914 --> 00:16:38.547
By comparison, a clean sheet showed only 3.4.

00:16:38.547 --> 00:16:43.080
It is obvious that sheets and covers should never be whisked into the air.

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:49.080
When sheets are changed, relatively few bacteria are scattered if the sheets are loosened and rolled up.

00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:55.740
Soiled linen must be collected in a bag and not removed until it is put in the washer.

00:16:56.720 --> 00:17:02.160
If soiled linen is carelessly handled, the trail of bacteria is not hard to imagine.

00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:16.140
If sorting of linen is done before washing, the air becomes heavily loaded, for when the bacteria multiply

00:17:16.147 --> 00:17:22.180
in the moist linens, the concentration becomes enormous.

00:17:22.181 --> 00:17:27.947
These airborne organisms may contaminate freshly washed linen and be redistributed to the wards.

00:17:27.947 --> 00:17:33.747
Also they are an occupational hazard for the laundry worker.

00:17:33.747 --> 00:17:41.913
Soiled linen should remain in the bags in which it has been collected.

00:17:41.914 --> 00:17:49.247
The laundry is then washed just as it comes from the wards. Sorting can be done after tumbling.

00:17:49.247 --> 00:18:01.247
This means that dressings and other trash must be kept out of laundry bags.

00:18:01.247 --> 00:18:07.947
A germicidal textile lubricant added to the final rinse will prevent the multiplication of organisms,

00:18:07.947 --> 00:18:13.247
as shown by the culture of treated and untreated linen swatches.

00:18:13.247 --> 00:18:17.147
The area of inhibition around the two treated swatches shows

00:18:17.147 --> 00:18:23.547
the germicidal effectiveness of the linen.

00:18:23.547 --> 00:18:27.760
Clean linen should be redistributed in covered laundry trucks

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:33.920
and never in those used for collection of soiled linen.

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:43.114
Sanitary processing during the laundry cycle can eliminate a possibly serious trail of infection.

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:47.940
Another reservoir in which bacteria await the next patient is the mattress.

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:55.814
Plastic covers protect mattresses and pillows and make disinfection with a long-acting germicide easy.

00:18:55.814 --> 00:19:01.814
Blankets present a more serious problem. This one, which was sterile when first put on,

00:19:01.814 --> 00:19:07.514
has been used by Mrs. Allen for seven days.

00:19:07.514 --> 00:19:12.947
Her organisms will contact the next patient unless the blanket is again sterilized.

00:19:15.681 --> 00:19:22.647
Another reservoir for bacteria, usually unsuspected, is bedside drinking water.

00:19:22.647 --> 00:19:28.120
When a patient drinks directly from a carafe or from a cup which also serves as the lid,

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:35.440
saliva contaminates the remaining water and bacteria are free to multiply.

00:19:35.447 --> 00:19:39.880
When this carafe is collected with others to be refilled and redistributed,

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:43.860
another spreader is set loose in the hospital.

00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:51.460
One solution is the use of disposable liners.

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:06.880
Tasteless, odorless germicide should be added to the water supply of icemakers

00:20:07.100 --> 00:20:10.700
to maintain purity of the cubes during distribution.

00:20:10.720 --> 00:20:13.840
Carafes should be filled directly from a water tap.

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:21.720
There are many other articles in the hospital that may be responsible

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:24.340
for transmitting bacteria from a septic patient.

00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:34.240
Some are so obvious that we routinely take special precautions.

00:20:34.247 --> 00:20:41.647
Others such as soap however, are rarely suspect, though soap is actually a nutrient medium

00:20:41.647 --> 00:20:46.980
and can become a hazardous feedback.

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:52.380
The air itself is still another source of trouble.

00:20:53.060 --> 00:20:58.940
Even if it were possible to isolate all septic patients behind walls, many hospitals are so designed

00:20:58.947 --> 00:21:05.613
that it is difficult to isolate patients from each other's air and airborne bacteria.

00:21:05.620 --> 00:21:10.820
Air, which is often recirculated, would carry Mrs. Allen's organisms to any room

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:13.300
serviced by the system.

00:21:14.480 --> 00:21:17.740
But beyond this simple spreading of bacteria,

00:21:17.747 --> 00:21:21.347
an untended air conditioner can also become a multiplier.

00:21:21.347 --> 00:21:28.780
Bacteria grow in the moisture on the refrigerator coils or in the humidifying pan.

00:21:28.780 --> 00:21:34.160
A few organisms going in may return after a time as a cloud.

00:21:40.981 --> 00:21:49.480
This feedback can be checked by regular maintenance. Baffles tend to accumulate slime of bacteria.

00:21:49.481 --> 00:21:54.447
They should be washed periodically with a detergent germicide.

00:21:54.447 --> 00:22:00.113
Also a bacterial agent should be added to the humidifying water. This treatment prevents an air conditioner

00:22:00.114 --> 00:22:05.614
from becoming a multiplier, though any system that recirculates air

00:22:05.614 --> 00:22:11.247
may broadcast the available organisms.

00:22:11.247 --> 00:22:16.713
The whims of air currents, however are not as simple as this.

00:22:16.714 --> 00:22:22.880
Ventilating systems distribute air under positive or negative pressure.

00:22:22.880 --> 00:22:28.140
Open doors and windows cause it to flow unpredictably.

00:22:28.980 --> 00:22:36.114
If doorways are left open in stairwells, a convection chimney forms.

00:22:36.114 --> 00:22:45.747
Elevator shafts, dumbwaiter shafts and laundry chutes add their bit, chiefly through piston action.

00:22:45.747 --> 00:22:49.880
The air current thus created, here shown by the movement of smoke,

00:22:49.881 --> 00:22:54.347
may be carried through an open door.

00:22:54.347 --> 00:23:01.747
It may pass over soiled linen, down the hall, up an open stairwell.

00:23:01.747 --> 00:23:07.080
The current may carry its bacteria to areas where they are a real hazard.

00:23:10.114 --> 00:23:16.947
The problems of controlling air currents are different in each hospital.

00:23:16.947 --> 00:23:22.613
Some can be solved by a little care.

00:23:22.614 --> 00:23:27.780
Some may require readjustment of ventilating equipment or hospital design.

00:23:27.781 --> 00:23:31.881
Not all of our sepsis problems are inanimate however.

00:23:31.881 --> 00:23:37.081
The human part of our environment perhaps presents the most complex problem of all.

00:23:37.081 --> 00:23:45.181
We are all colonized. We all scatter organisms from our noses, our skins, and our perineum.

00:23:45.181 --> 00:23:48.881
Some of us, though apparently in good health, become multipliers,

00:23:48.881 --> 00:23:52.447
and a source of serious infection to others.

00:23:52.447 --> 00:23:57.580
Such individuals certainly must not work where they can endanger patients.

00:23:57.581 --> 00:24:02.614
They have no place among hospital personnel.

00:24:02.614 --> 00:24:07.814
There are those who may have active skin infections.

00:24:07.814 --> 00:24:14.714
They, too, should absent themselves while their infection is communicable.

00:24:14.714 --> 00:24:21.014
Others who do not have infections themselves may spread bacteria from patient to patient.

00:24:21.014 --> 00:24:27.947
This could be an orderly who moments before was emptying soiled laundry.

00:24:27.947 --> 00:24:34.080
The number of bacteria scattered from a healthy nasopharynx is of no great significance,

00:24:34.620 --> 00:24:40.560
but when the respiratory tract becomes diseased, bacteria multiply several thousand percent.

00:24:45.340 --> 00:24:54.260
Here is a sneeze in slow motion, the person has a cold.

00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:00.920
Masks on a healthy person may be adequate,

00:25:01.060 --> 00:25:04.914
though typical masks are only about ten percent efficient.

00:25:04.914 --> 00:25:10.347
When a person has an upper respiratory infection, masks are totally inadequate.

00:25:10.347 --> 00:25:15.920
Mask soaked by perspiration are no barriers to a cough or sneeze.

00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:28.040
These are some of the problems that every hospital faces,

00:25:28.540 --> 00:25:33.400
problems introduced by the patient infected with resistant organisms.

00:25:34.280 --> 00:25:42.380
It is not hard to recognize bacteria in a drawing as they're spread by a mop.

00:25:42.381 --> 00:25:49.614
It is easy to eliminate a carrier by fading her out of a drawing.

00:25:49.614 --> 00:25:56.214
It is quite simple to state that we must use care.

00:25:56.214 --> 00:26:01.147
It is not so simple, however, to deal with these complex practical problems

00:26:01.147 --> 00:26:06.280
or even to see the septic ferments in our own hospitals.

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:14.040
But once we realize what our problems are, the trails across infection can be stopped.

00:26:15.020 --> 00:26:23.020
We can regulate pails and mops, we can supervise soiled linen, we can sterilize blankets and [?],

00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:28.440
we can control ventilation, drinking water, each item that leaves the septic room.

00:26:28.820 --> 00:26:32.280
We can emphasize handwashing and personal cleanliness.

00:26:32.281 --> 00:26:36.881
We can develop ways of isolating the infected patient.

00:26:36.881 --> 00:26:40.881
The techniques are available.

00:26:40.881 --> 00:26:47.380
This is an overall hospital problem. Everyone contributes.

00:26:47.720 --> 00:26:53.414
Each of us must be willing to supervise constantly and to be supervised.

00:26:53.414 --> 00:27:00.514
And above all, each of us must be able to see with his mental eye the septic ferments.

00:27:00.520 --> 00:27:23.800
[Music]

